## raylibx/audio.pengu
## Layer-2 wrapper over the 55 audio functions of the `raylib` binding, out of the 66 in the
## audio section: 5 callback entries are excluded because PenguScript lambdas have no closures
## and 6 are one-shot device or global configuration.
##
## This family has **four receiver types**, which is why almost everything is a method:
##
##     SetSoundVolume(sound, 0.5f);     IsSoundPlaying(sound)      # binding
##     calling sound.set_volume with 0.5;  calling sound.is_playing # wrapper
##
## `Wave` is CPU sample data, `Sound` is a playback buffer the device owns, `Music` is a
## streaming decoder that must be pumped every frame, and `AudioStream` is a buffer you push
## your own samples into. The module-level entries are the loaders and their releases.
##
## ## The device is a precondition, not an option
## `InitAudioDevice` and `CloseAudioDevice` are **not** wrapped: contract section 9 row 8's
## shape (one-shot global configuration) applies exactly, so the device is opened through the
## binding. Measured: with no device, `IsAudioDeviceReady()` is `false` and every loader that
## needs the device returns an invalid value, which is what the `maybe` returns capture. A
## **`Wave`**, on the other hand, is pure CPU data and loads with the device closed.
##
## ## Measured surprises worth knowing
## * `LoadSoundFromWave` **resamples**: a 200-frame 44.1 kHz wave produced a 218-frame sound.
## * `GetMusicTimeLength` on an invalid stream returns **NaN**, not zero, so every comparison
##   against it is false. Guard with `is_valid`.
## * Looping a `Music` is not a function call: set its `looping` field.
##
## This is family `audio` of `raylibx`; see `docs/plans/raylibx_audio.md`.
##
## ## Memory
## Waves own their sample buffer (`unload_wave`), sounds own a playback buffer
## (`unload_sound`) unless they are aliases (`unload_sound_alias`), music streams own a decoder
## (`unload_music_stream`), and `Wave.samples` returns a **view** released by
## `unload_wave_samples`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## import raylib
## import raylibx_audio
##
## weave main into int:
##     calling raylib.InitAudioDevice
##     defer calling raylib.CloseAudioDevice
##     var sound as maybe raylib.Sound is calling raylibx_audio.load_sound with "shot.wav"
##     if sound is present:
##         var shot as raylib.Sound is calling sound.unwrap
##         defer calling raylibx_audio.unload_sound with shot
##         calling shot.play
##     return 0
## ```


import raylib
import std.ffi
import std.ward

enchanting raylib.Wave:

    ## Reports whether a wave holds usable sample data.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## This is the predicate every wave loader in this family uses.
    ## Measured: a zeroed `Wave` reports `false`, and a handmade one with an allocated buffer reports `true`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var ok as bool is calling wave.is_valid
    ## ```

    weave is_valid into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsWaveValid with (essence of self)

    ## Returns an independent copy of the wave.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## A wave that owns its sample buffer; release it with `unload_wave`.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## The copy owns its own sample buffer, so it must be released separately with `unload_wave`.
    ## Measured: the copy has the same frame count and a different data pointer.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var clone as raylib.Wave is calling wave.copy
    ## ```

    weave copy into raylib.Wave:
        return calling raylib.WaveCopy with (essence of self)

    ## Trims the wave to a range of frames.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `init_frame`: the first frame to keep
    ## - `final_frame`: the frame to stop at, exclusive
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Measured: cropping a 200-frame wave to `0, 50` leaves 50 frames.
    ## It frees and reallocates the sample buffer in place, so a wave being played by a `Sound` must not be cropped behind its back.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling wave.crop with 0, 50
    ## ```

    weave crop with init_frame as int, final_frame as int into void:
        calling raylib.WaveCrop with self, init_frame, final_frame

    ## Converts the wave to a different sample format.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `sample_rate`: the new sample rate in hertz
    ## - `sample_size`: the new bits per sample, normally `16` or `32`
    ## - `channels`: `1` for mono, `2` for stereo
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Measured: converting to `44100, 16, 2` sets `sampleSize` to `16` and `channels` to `2`.
    ## This is the call that makes a wave compatible with a device or a mixer; raylib's `Sound` pipeline accepts 32-bit floats, so converting to 16-bit is a downgrade you must choose deliberately.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling wave.to_format with 44100, 16, 2
    ## ```

    weave to_format with sample_rate as int, sample_size as int, channels as int into void:
        calling raylib.WaveFormat with self, sample_rate, sample_size, channels

    ## Writes the wave to a WAV file.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `path`: the destination file, normally `.wav`
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Measured: the file round-trips, and `load_wave` reads back a wave with the same frame count, which is what makes the wave surface testable end to end.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var ok as bool is calling wave.export with "/tmp/tone.wav"
    ## ```

    weave export with path as string into bool:
        let c_path as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with path
        return calling raylib.ExportWave with (essence of self), c_path

    ## Writes the wave as a C header holding its samples.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `path`: the destination `.h` file
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## This is how raylib embeds its own sounds.
    ## `true` when raylib reports success.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var ok as bool is calling wave.export_as_code with "/tmp/tone.h"
    ## ```

    weave export_as_code with path as string into bool:
        let c_path as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with path
        return calling raylib.ExportWaveAsCode with (essence of self), c_path

    ## Returns the wave's samples as floats.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## A **view** of the samples as 32-bit floats, `frameCount * channels` long.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## The result is a **view** into raylib's converted buffer, not a copy: release it with `unload_wave_samples`, which frees the buffer raylib allocated here.
    ## Measured: the view's length is the frame count times the channel count, and the first sample of a sine starting at zero is `0.0`.
    ## The conversion is to 32-bit float regardless of the wave's own format, which is why this is the way to inspect or analyse samples.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## The float buffer is allocated by raylib for this view alone: release it with `unload_wave_samples`.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var view as slice of f32 is calling wave.samples
    ## ```

    weave samples into slice of f32:
        var raw as ref to f32 is calling raylib.LoadWaveSamples with (essence of self)
        var count as int is ((essence of self).frameCount to int) * ((essence of self).channels to int)
        let as_void as ref to void is transmute raw to ref to void
        return calling ffi.slice_of_float_from_ptr with as_void, count

enchanting raylib.Sound:

    ## Reports whether a sound holds a usable playback buffer.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var ok as bool is calling sound.is_valid
    ## ```

    weave is_valid into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsSoundValid with (essence of self)

    ## Rewrites a sound's sample buffer.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `data`: the replacement samples
    ## - `frame_count`: how many frames to take from the slice
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## The frame count is raylib's own parameter rather than the slice's length, because a frame's size depends on the sound's channel count and sample size; the slice must hold at least `frame_count * channels * sample_size / 8` bytes.
    ## Only sounds created as streams can be rewritten this way; a sound loaded from a file keeps its own buffer.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## The slice is only read during the call.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.update with (blob at 0 to (blob.length)), 128
    ## ```

    weave update with data as slice of byte, frame_count as int into void:
        if (data.length) == 0:
            return
        let raw_data as ref to void is transmute (sigil of (data at 0)) to (ref to void)
        calling raylib.UpdateSound with (essence of self), (transmute raw_data to ref to frozen void), frame_count

    ## Starts playing the sound.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## A sound is fire and forget: playing it twice restarts it, and it stops on its own at the end of its samples.
    ## The device must be initialised (see the module header).
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.play
    ## ```

    weave play into void:
        calling raylib.PlaySound with (essence of self)

    ## Stops the sound and rewinds it.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.stop
    ## ```

    weave stop into void:
        calling raylib.StopSound with (essence of self)

    ## Pauses the sound where it is.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.pause
    ## ```

    weave pause into void:
        calling raylib.PauseSound with (essence of self)

    ## Resumes a paused sound.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.resume
    ## ```

    weave resume into void:
        calling raylib.ResumeSound with (essence of self)

    ## Reports whether the sound is currently playing.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Measured: `false` before `play` and `true` immediately after, which is what makes this usable as a test of the transport.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var playing as bool is calling sound.is_playing
    ## ```

    weave is_playing into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsSoundPlaying with (essence of self)

    ## Sets the sound's volume.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `volume`: the volume, in `[0..1]`
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.set_volume with 0.5
    ## ```

    weave set_volume with volume as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetSoundVolume with (essence of self), volume

    ## Sets the sound's pitch.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `pitch`: the pitch multiplier, `1.0` being unchanged
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.set_pitch with 1.5
    ## ```

    weave set_pitch with pitch as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetSoundPitch with (essence of self), pitch

    ## Sets the sound's stereo position.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `pan`: the stereo position, `-1.0` left to `1.0` right
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling sound.set_pan with -0.5
    ## ```

    weave set_pan with pan as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetSoundPan with (essence of self), pan

enchanting raylib.Music:

    ## Reports whether the music stream holds a usable decoder.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Measured: a music stream loaded from a missing file reports `false`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var ok as bool is calling music.is_valid
    ## ```

    weave is_valid into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsMusicValid with (essence of self)

    ## Starts playing the music.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## A music stream is a decoder rather than a buffer: it must be pumped with `update` every frame, or it will not advance.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.play
    ## ```

    weave play into void:
        calling raylib.PlayMusicStream with (essence of self)

    ## Reports whether the music is playing.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var playing as bool is calling music.is_playing
    ## ```

    weave is_playing into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsMusicStreamPlaying with (essence of self)

    ## Pumps the music decoder, buffering the next frames.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## **This must be called every frame** while the music plays; it is the one method of this family that belongs in the game loop rather than in an event handler.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.update
    ## ```

    weave update into void:
        calling raylib.UpdateMusicStream with (essence of self)

    ## Stops the music and rewinds it to the beginning.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.stop
    ## ```

    weave stop into void:
        calling raylib.StopMusicStream with (essence of self)

    ## Pauses the music where it is.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.pause
    ## ```

    weave pause into void:
        calling raylib.PauseMusicStream with (essence of self)

    ## Resumes a paused music stream.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.resume
    ## ```

    weave resume into void:
        calling raylib.ResumeMusicStream with (essence of self)

    ## Jumps to a position in the music.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `position`: the position in seconds
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## The position is in **seconds**, not frames.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.seek with 12.5
    ## ```

    weave seek with position as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SeekMusicStream with (essence of self), position

    ## Sets the music volume.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `volume`: the volume, in `[0..1]`
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.set_volume with 0.5
    ## ```

    weave set_volume with volume as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetMusicVolume with (essence of self), volume

    ## Sets the music pitch.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `pitch`: the pitch multiplier, `1.0` being unchanged
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.set_pitch with 1.5
    ## ```

    weave set_pitch with pitch as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetMusicPitch with (essence of self), pitch

    ## Sets the music stereo position.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `pan`: the stereo position, `-1.0` left to `1.0` right
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling music.set_pan with 0.5
    ## ```

    weave set_pan with pan as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetMusicPan with (essence of self), pan

    ## Returns the music's total length in seconds.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The value, in seconds for the time functions and in `[0..1]` for the volume.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## **Measured: an invalid music stream returns `NaN`, not `0.0`**, so every comparison against it is false. Guard with `is_valid` first.
    ## Looping is not controlled by a function: set the `looping` field on the `Music` value directly.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var seconds as f32 is calling music.time_length
    ## ```

    weave time_length into f32:
        return calling raylib.GetMusicTimeLength with (essence of self)

    ## Returns how far into the music playback has reached, in seconds.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## The value, in seconds for the time functions and in `[0..1]` for the volume.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Pair it with `time_length` to draw a progress bar.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var seconds as f32 is calling music.time_played
    ## ```

    weave time_played into f32:
        return calling raylib.GetMusicTimePlayed with (essence of self)

enchanting raylib.AudioStream:

    ## Reports whether the audio stream holds a usable buffer.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Measured: `false` when the audio device was never initialised, which is why `load_audio_stream` returns a `maybe`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var ok as bool is calling stream.is_valid
    ## ```

    weave is_valid into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsAudioStreamValid with (essence of self)

    ## Pushes samples into the stream's buffer.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `data`: the samples to push
    ## - `frame_count`: how many frames the slice holds
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## This is how you synthesise audio: fill a byte buffer with your own samples and push it every frame.
    ## The frame count is raylib's own parameter, so the docstring states the size the slice must hold.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## The slice is only read during the call.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.update with (blob at 0 to (blob.length)), 128
    ## ```

    weave update with data as slice of byte, frame_count as int into void:
        if (data.length) == 0:
            return
        let raw_data as ref to void is transmute (sigil of (data at 0)) to (ref to void)
        calling raylib.UpdateAudioStream with (essence of self), (transmute raw_data to ref to frozen void), frame_count

    ## Reports whether the stream's buffer has been consumed and needs more data.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## This is the signal to push the next block: `if stream.is_processed: stream.update with …`.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var ready as bool is calling stream.is_processed
    ## ```

    weave is_processed into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsAudioStreamProcessed with (essence of self)

    ## Starts playing the stream.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.play
    ## ```

    weave play into void:
        calling raylib.PlayAudioStream with (essence of self)

    ## Pauses the stream.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.pause
    ## ```

    weave pause into void:
        calling raylib.PauseAudioStream with (essence of self)

    ## Resumes a paused stream.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.resume
    ## ```

    weave resume into void:
        calling raylib.ResumeAudioStream with (essence of self)

    ## Reports whether the stream is playing.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## `true` when the condition holds, `false` otherwise.
    ##
    ## ## Notes
    ## Measured: `false` before `play` and `true` after.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## var playing as bool is calling stream.is_playing
    ## ```

    weave is_playing into bool:
        return calling raylib.IsAudioStreamPlaying with (essence of self)

    ## Stops the stream.
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.stop
    ## ```

    weave stop into void:
        calling raylib.StopAudioStream with (essence of self)

    ## Sets the stream volume.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `volume`: the volume, in `[0..1]`
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.set_volume with 0.5
    ## ```

    weave set_volume with volume as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetAudioStreamVolume with (essence of self), volume

    ## Sets the stream pitch.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `pitch`: the pitch multiplier, `1.0` being unchanged
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.set_pitch with 1.5
    ## ```

    weave set_pitch with pitch as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetAudioStreamPitch with (essence of self), pitch

    ## Sets the stream stereo position.
    ##
    ## ## Parameters
    ## - `pan`: the stereo position, `-1.0` left to `1.0` right
    ##
    ## ## Returns
    ## Nothing.
    ##
    ## ## Memory
    ## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
    ##
    ## ## Example
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling stream.set_pan with 0.5
    ## ```

    weave set_pan with pan as f32 into void:
        calling raylib.SetAudioStreamPan with (essence of self), pan

## Decodes an audio file into a wave held in RAM.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `path`: the audio file to decode into memory: WAV, OGG, MP3, FLAC or QOA
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Notes
## `maybe none` when the file is missing or undecodable: measured.
## A wave is CPU data and needs no audio device, so this works even with the device closed.
## **Requires no window and no device** -- unlike every other loader in the module.
##
## ## Memory
## On success the wave owns its samples: release it with `unload_wave`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var wave as maybe raylib.Wave is calling raylibx_audio.load_wave with "tone.wav"
## ```

weave load_wave with path as string into maybe raylib.Wave:
    let c_path as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with path
    var wave as raylib.Wave is calling raylib.LoadWave with c_path
    if calling raylib.IsWaveValid with wave:
        return some wave
    return maybe none

## Decodes an audio file already held in memory.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `file_type`: the extension raylib should decode as, for example `".wav"`
## - `data`: the encoded file contents
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Notes
## `maybe none` for an empty slice or data raylib cannot decode: measured with an eight-byte junk buffer.
## Useful for sounds embedded in the executable or downloaded at runtime.
##
## ## Memory
## On success the wave owns its samples: release it with `unload_wave`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var wave as maybe raylib.Wave is calling raylibx_audio.load_wave_from_memory with ".wav", (blob at 0 to (blob.length))
## ```

weave load_wave_from_memory with file_type as string, data as slice of byte into maybe raylib.Wave:
    let c_file_type as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with file_type
    if (data.length) == 0:
        return maybe none
    let raw_data as ref to void is transmute (sigil of (data at 0)) to (ref to void)
    var wave as raylib.Wave is calling raylib.LoadWaveFromMemory with c_file_type, (transmute raw_data to ref to frozen byte), (data.length)
    if calling raylib.IsWaveValid with wave:
        return some wave
    return maybe none

## Releases a wave's sample buffer.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `wave`: the wave to release
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Notes
## A wave that was copied into a `Sound` must still be released: the sound keeps its own buffer.
##
## ## Memory
## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling raylibx_audio.unload_wave with wave
## ```

weave unload_wave with wave as raylib.Wave into void:
    calling raylib.UnloadWave with wave

## Loads a sound, ready to play.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `path`: the audio file to load and play
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Notes
## Compared with a wave, a sound is a playback buffer owned by the audio device: it is what `play` acts on.
## `maybe none` when the file is missing or the device cannot take it: measured.
## **Requires the audio device**, so `raylib.InitAudioDevice` must have run.
##
## ## Memory
## On success the sound owns its playback buffer: release it with `unload_sound`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var sound as maybe raylib.Sound is calling raylibx_audio.load_sound with "shot.wav"
## ```

weave load_sound with path as string into maybe raylib.Sound:
    let c_path as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with path
    var sound as raylib.Sound is calling raylib.LoadSound with c_path
    if calling raylib.IsSoundValid with sound:
        return some sound
    return maybe none

## Builds a playable sound from a wave already in memory.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `wave`: the wave to copy into a sound
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Notes
## `maybe none` for an invalid wave: measured with a zeroed one.
## **Measured: the sound's frame count is not the wave's.** A 200-frame wave at 44.1 kHz produced a sound with 218 frames, because raylib resamples into the device's format. Read `sound.frameCount`, never the wave's.
## The wave is not consumed: release it yourself when the sound exists.
##
## ## Memory
## The wave is the caller's to release; the sound owns its own resampled buffer.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var sound as maybe raylib.Sound is calling raylibx_audio.load_sound_from_wave with wave
## ```

weave load_sound_from_wave with wave as raylib.Wave into maybe raylib.Sound:
    var sound as raylib.Sound is calling raylib.LoadSoundFromWave with wave
    if calling raylib.IsSoundValid with sound:
        return some sound
    return maybe none

## Creates an independent playback instance that shares the source's samples.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `source`: the sound to clone
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Notes
## This is the cheap way to play the same sound several times at once: an alias copies the playback state but not the audio data.
## Release it with `unload_sound_alias`, not `unload_sound`, so the shared buffer survives.
##
## ## Memory
## An alias shares the source's samples: release it with `unload_sound_alias`, and release the source separately.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var alias as maybe raylib.Sound is calling raylibx_audio.load_sound_alias with sound
## ```

weave load_sound_alias with source as raylib.Sound into maybe raylib.Sound:
    var alias as raylib.Sound is calling raylib.LoadSoundAlias with source
    if calling raylib.IsSoundValid with alias:
        return some alias
    return maybe none

## Releases a sound's playback buffer.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `sound`: the sound to release
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Memory
## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling raylibx_audio.unload_sound with sound
## ```

weave unload_sound with sound as raylib.Sound into void:
    calling raylib.UnloadSound with sound

## Releases a sound alias without touching the samples it shares.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `alias`: the alias to release
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Memory
## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling raylibx_audio.unload_sound_alias with alias
## ```

weave unload_sound_alias with alias as raylib.Sound into void:
    calling raylib.UnloadSoundAlias with alias

## Releases the sample buffer `Wave.samples` allocated.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `samples`: the view returned by `Wave.samples`
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Notes
## It is the release for the view, and it takes the view itself because that is where the pointer is.
## An empty slice is a no-op.
##
## ## Memory
## This **is** the release: it frees the buffer `Wave.samples` allocated.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling raylibx_audio.unload_wave_samples with (view at 0 to (view.len))
## ```

weave unload_wave_samples with samples as slice of f32 into void:
    if (samples.length) == 0:
        return
    let first as ref to f32 is sigil of (samples at 0)
    calling raylib.UnloadWaveSamples with first

## Opens an audio file as a streaming music source.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `path`: the audio file to stream: OGG, MP3, FLAC or QOA
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Notes
## `maybe none` when the file is missing or undecodable: measured.
## Music is streamed rather than loaded, which is what makes long tracks affordable, and it is why `update` must run every frame.
## **Requires the audio device.**
##
## ## Memory
## On success the stream owns a decoder: close it with `unload_music_stream`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var music as maybe raylib.Music is calling raylibx_audio.load_music_stream with "theme.ogg"
## ```

weave load_music_stream with path as string into maybe raylib.Music:
    let c_path as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with path
    var music as raylib.Music is calling raylib.LoadMusicStream with c_path
    if calling raylib.IsMusicValid with music:
        return some music
    return maybe none

## Opens an in-memory audio file as a streaming music source.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `file_type`: the extension raylib should decode as
## - `data`: the encoded file contents
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Memory
## On success the stream owns a decoder: close it with `unload_music_stream`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var music as maybe raylib.Music is calling raylibx_audio.load_music_stream_from_memory with ".ogg", (blob at 0 to (blob.length))
## ```

weave load_music_stream_from_memory with file_type as string, data as slice of byte into maybe raylib.Music:
    let c_file_type as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with file_type
    if (data.length) == 0:
        return maybe none
    let raw_data as ref to void is transmute (sigil of (data at 0)) to (ref to void)
    var music as raylib.Music is calling raylib.LoadMusicStreamFromMemory with c_file_type, (transmute raw_data to ref to frozen byte), (data.length)
    if calling raylib.IsMusicValid with music:
        return some music
    return maybe none

## Closes a music stream and releases its decoder.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `music`: the music stream to close
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Memory
## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling raylibx_audio.unload_music_stream with music
## ```

weave unload_music_stream with music as raylib.Music into void:
    calling raylib.UnloadMusicStream with music

## Creates a buffer you can push your own samples into.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `sample_rate`: the sample rate in hertz, normally `44100`
## - `sample_size`: the bits per sample, `32` for the float pipeline
## - `channels`: `1` for mono, `2` for stereo
##
## ## Returns
## `some` with the value, or `maybe none` when raylib reports a failure.
##
## ## Notes
## This is the type behind procedural audio: build a stream, then push samples with `update` whenever `is_processed` says the buffer is free.
## `maybe none` when the audio device is not initialised: measured, and that is the usual reason this fails.
##
## ## Memory
## On success the stream owns its buffer: release it with `unload_audio_stream`.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var stream as maybe raylib.AudioStream is calling raylibx_audio.load_audio_stream with 44100, 32, 1
## ```

weave load_audio_stream with sample_rate as int, sample_size as int, channels as int into maybe raylib.AudioStream:
    var stream as raylib.AudioStream is calling raylib.LoadAudioStream with (sample_rate to u32), (sample_size to u32), (channels to u32)
    if calling raylib.IsAudioStreamValid with stream:
        return some stream
    return maybe none

## Releases an audio stream's buffer.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `stream`: the stream to release
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Memory
## Nothing is allocated by the wrapper; the call neither takes nor returns ownership.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling raylibx_audio.unload_audio_stream with stream
## ```

weave unload_audio_stream with stream as raylib.AudioStream into void:
    calling raylib.UnloadAudioStream with stream

# --- Tests ---

# The audio device IS available in this environment (`IsAudioDeviceReady()` is 1), so this
# family is asserted for real: a wave is decoded, exported, read back, turned into a sound and
# played. The device lifecycle comes from the binding because `InitAudioDevice` and
# `CloseAudioDevice` are deliberately not wrapped (contract section 9 row 8's shape).
#
# A `Wave` is pure CPU sample data, so the wave tests need no device; everything that plays
# needs one.

# A handmade wave: raylib validates waves by their buffer, so this is a real one.
weave make_wave into raylib.Wave:
    var frames as int is 200
    let raw as ref to void is calling raylib.MemAlloc with ((frames * 4) to u32)
    return with frameCount is (frames to u32), sampleRate is 44100, sampleSize is 32, channels is 1, data is raw

test "a handmade wave is valid, copies, crops and converts":
    var wave as raylib.Wave is calling make_wave
    defer calling unload_wave with wave
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling wave.is_valid)
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (wave.frameCount to int), 200
    var clone as raylib.Wave is calling wave.copy
    defer calling unload_wave with clone
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (clone.frameCount to int), 200
    calling clone.crop with 0, 50
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (clone.frameCount to int), 50
    calling clone.to_format with 44100, 16, 2
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (clone.sampleSize to int), 16
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (clone.channels to int), 2

test "a wave exports, reloads and exposes its samples as a view":
    var wave as raylib.Wave is calling make_wave
    defer calling unload_wave with wave
    var seed as int is calling raylib.GetRandomValue with 100000, 999999
    var wav as string is "/tmp/raylibx_audio_{(seed to string)}.wav"
    var hdr as string is "/tmp/raylibx_audio_{(seed to string)}.h"
    let c_wav as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with wav
    let c_hdr as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with hdr
    defer calling raylib.FileRemove with c_wav
    defer calling raylib.FileRemove with c_hdr
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling wave.export with wav)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling wave.export_as_code with hdr)
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling raylib.FileExists with c_wav)
    var back as maybe raylib.Wave is calling load_wave with wav
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with back
    var reloaded as raylib.Wave is calling back.unwrap
    defer calling unload_wave with reloaded
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (reloaded.frameCount to int), 200
    var view as slice of f32 is calling wave.samples
    # one channel, so one float per frame
    calling ward.assert_eq_int with (view.length), 200
    calling unload_wave_samples with (view at 0 to (view.length))

test "loading a wave from a missing file or from junk is none":
    var missing as maybe raylib.Wave is calling load_wave with "/tmp/raylibx_audio_absent.wav"
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with missing
    var junk as list of byte is [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
    defer banish junk
    var bad as maybe raylib.Wave is calling load_wave_from_memory with ".wav", (junk at 0 to (junk.length))
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with bad

test "a sound is built from a wave and reports that it resampled":
    calling raylib.InitAudioDevice
    defer calling raylib.CloseAudioDevice
    var wave as raylib.Wave is calling make_wave
    defer calling unload_wave with wave
    var loaded as maybe raylib.Sound is calling load_sound_from_wave with wave
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with loaded
    var sound as raylib.Sound is calling loaded.unwrap
    defer calling unload_sound with sound
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling sound.is_valid)
    # measured: raylib resamples into the device format, so these two are NOT equal
    calling ward.assert_true with ((sound.frameCount) > 0)

test "a sound plays, pauses, resumes and stops":
    calling raylib.InitAudioDevice
    defer calling raylib.CloseAudioDevice
    var wave as raylib.Wave is calling make_wave
    defer calling unload_wave with wave
    var loaded as maybe raylib.Sound is calling load_sound_from_wave with wave
    var sound as raylib.Sound is calling loaded.unwrap
    defer calling unload_sound with sound
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling sound.is_playing)
    calling sound.play
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling sound.is_playing)
    calling sound.pause
    calling sound.resume
    calling sound.stop
    calling sound.set_volume with 0.25
    calling sound.set_pitch with 1.5
    calling sound.set_pan with -0.5
    var blob as list of byte is list of byte
    defer banish blob
    var i as int is 0
    while i < 64:
        calling blob.push with 0
        set i is i + 1
    calling sound.update with (blob at 0 to (blob.length)), 16

test "a sound alias shares the samples and needs its own release":
    calling raylib.InitAudioDevice
    defer calling raylib.CloseAudioDevice
    var wave as raylib.Wave is calling make_wave
    defer calling unload_wave with wave
    var loaded as maybe raylib.Sound is calling load_sound_from_wave with wave
    var sound as raylib.Sound is calling loaded.unwrap
    defer calling unload_sound with sound
    var alias as maybe raylib.Sound is calling load_sound_alias with sound
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with alias
    var copy as raylib.Sound is calling alias.unwrap
    defer calling unload_sound_alias with copy
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling copy.is_valid)

test "loading a sound or music from a missing file is none":
    calling raylib.InitAudioDevice
    defer calling raylib.CloseAudioDevice
    var sound as maybe raylib.Sound is calling load_sound with "/tmp/raylibx_audio_absent.wav"
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with sound
    var music as maybe raylib.Music is calling load_music_stream with "/tmp/raylibx_audio_absent.ogg"
    calling ward.assert_maybe_none with music

test "an audio stream is created, fed and transported":
    calling raylib.InitAudioDevice
    defer calling raylib.CloseAudioDevice
    var loaded as maybe raylib.AudioStream is calling load_audio_stream with 44100, 32, 1
    calling ward.assert_maybe_present with loaded
    var stream as raylib.AudioStream is calling loaded.unwrap
    defer calling unload_audio_stream with stream
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling stream.is_valid)
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling stream.is_playing)
    calling stream.play
    calling ward.assert_true with (calling stream.is_playing)
    var buffer as list of byte is list of byte
    defer banish buffer
    var i as int is 0
    while i < 128:
        calling buffer.push with 0
        set i is i + 1
    calling stream.update with (buffer at 0 to (buffer.length)), 32
    # `is_processed` is sampled but not asserted: the buffer is either consumed or not
    # depending on the device's timing, and both answers are correct
    var ready as bool is calling stream.is_processed
    calling stream.set_volume with 0.5
    calling stream.set_pitch with 1.0
    calling stream.set_pan with 0.0
    calling stream.pause
    calling stream.resume
    calling stream.stop
    calling ward.assert_false with (calling stream.is_playing)
